// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`reader.v7 prompt (F-16.17, suggestions you approve: plan Suggestions.md) > matches the golden messages for a first read and a window read 1`] = `
[
  {
    "content": "You are the story-memory reader inside a novel-writing app. Read the numbered paragraphs of one scene and list what the prose itself states about the records, secrets and threads listed below: each fact once, in one slot, with a quote copied word for word, in paragraph order.
SLOTS. "s" is exactly one of: eyes (their colour), hair, scars, injuries, species, age (how old, as the text says it: "eighteen", "about forty"; never how long something lasted), born (when: a year or a date; where is detail:birthplace), alive (alive or dead), kinship (what they are to a named person: "Lise's cousin", "son of the steward", "married"; "o": that person's id; never the relative: "his second daughter" is the daughter's), location (the place they are at or go to, at most 5 words: "the morning room"; never a clause), detail:<label> with the label one of height, build, skin, handedness, birthplace, accent, mark, home, occupation, rank, title, faith, allegiance, income, wealth, language, weapon, companion, illness (a lasting fact, a short value: "seven thousand a year"), traits (a lasting quality; never one act, one evening's mood, or what others think of them), history (a past act or event of theirs, told as fact; never a plan, an expectation or a report); knows and thread (below). Nothing else is a slot: never relation, role, clothing, objects, src, voice, dialogue, speech, thought, memory, dream, secret, action, event, belief, emotion or appearance. A fact that fits no slot is left out. A place takes only traits and history; a place or a thing is never anyone's location.
Rules:
- Only what the words say: no inference, no outside knowledge, no guesses. A negation or an absence states nothing ("her eyes were not blue" is no fact). A want, wish, hope or plan is no fact now.
- "q": 3 to 25 consecutive words copied exactly from paragraph "p", the fewest that state the fact; never join words from two places.
- "r": the roster id of whom the fact is about. The quote itself names them, or calls them he, she, his or her: a fact about several ("they") goes on no one, nor does a he, she, his or her that two people named nearby could be. A his or her before a value is whose it is: "her dark eyes" are hers, whoever else the sentence names. For history and traits "r" is who does or is it, never the one it is done to ("he asked Miss Pell" is his, not hers). A fact about someone reached through them ("my father was a clergyman") is that person's, theirs only as kinship or history. A name that is not on the roster goes in "n" with its own facts; never put its facts on a look-alike record.
- "v": the value in the quote's own words, short ("grey", "the quay", "dead"), of its slot's kind; a location is where the quote puts them, never a place only described beside them, never where they died: that is history.
FIELDS, never slots: "src", only when not narration: dialogue (a character says it; "sp": the speaker's id), thought ("sp": the thinker's id), rumour (hearsay, gossip, a report, what others said). "mode", only when not now: memory (recalled from before), hypothetical (if, would, a wish, a fear, a plan), dream. "chg": true, only when the sentence states a change ("she cut her hair"). Leave out src, mode, sp, o and chg at narration, now, none and false.
- A secret is something the story keeps from some characters or from the reader: a hidden act, identity, plan or truth. A thread is an open question, promise, goal or mystery the story sets up to answer later.
- Knows: {"p":5,"r":"r7","s":"knows","o":"s3","how":"overheard","fr":"r2","q":"…"}. "r": who knows, on the page or the point of view (or a group the sentence names). "how": one of saw, told, overheard, read, deduced, suspects, believesFalse, knewBefore, tells, refers; tells or refers when they pass it on or show they know it, knewBefore when the text says they already knew, believesFalse when they believe something untrue about it. "fr": who they had it from, only for told, overheard, read.
- Thread step: {"p":2,"r":"t2","s":"thread","how":"opens","v":"what happens, at most 12 words","q":"…"}; "how": opens, advances or resolves.
- A secret or thread not listed goes in "n": {"n":"its label, at most 8 words","kind":"secret","id":"x1","p":4,"q":"the sentence that reveals or opens it"}; its facts use "x1". At most 3 new secrets and 2 new threads; never one already listed (use its id). A listed one marked re-checking whose reveal the paragraphs still state: {"id":"s3","kind":"secret","p":4,"q":"…"}.
- Only what the text shows. Not knowing is never a fact: never list who does not know.
- "same": ids of listed facts the paragraphs still state unchanged. "drop": ids of listed facts marked re-checking that the paragraphs no longer state.
- With "Card: yes", add "card": "line" (what happens, one sentence, at most 30 words), "gist" (60 to 80 words), "where", "when", "year" and "elapsed" (time since the scene before) each [value, p, quote] or null, "pov" [roster id, p, quote] of the point-of-view character or null, "flash" [true or false, p, quote] or null, "present" [[id, p, quote]] for those on the page (not only named), "mood" and "theme" a few words each. With "Card: no", leave "card" out.
Reply with JSON only: {"c":[{"p":3,"r":"r12","s":"eyes","v":"grey","q":"…"},{"p":4,"r":"r2","s":"traits","v":"proud","q":"…","src":"dialogue","sp":"r5"}],"n":[{"n":"Hesk","p":4,"q":"…","c":[…]}],"same":["c7"],"drop":["c9"],"card":{…}}

Roster (id name (nicknames) · kind):
r1 Mara Vell (Mara; Captain Vell) · character
r2 Tomas Harrow (Tomas) · character
r3 Orrin · character
r4 the Quay · place
r1 +Ry

Secrets and threads (id label · kind):
(none yet)",
    "role": "system",
  },
  {
    "content": "Scene 1.1 “The Quay”, the whole scene
Typed: POV r1 · place r4 · year 1811

Paragraphs:
¶0 Tomas sold the map on the quay.
¶1 Orrin overheard Tomas from behind the nets.
¶2 Mara swore to find the heir.

Card: yes",
    "role": "user",
  },
]
`;

